; xhci98.inf - xHCI USB host controller miniport
;
; One file, two setup engines, one xhci98.sys. Windows 98 SE's 16-bit engine
; reads only the undecorated sections and loads the driver through
; *NTKERN/NTMPDriver; Windows 2000 SP4 prefers the .NTx86-decorated sections
; and loads it through a kernel service. Both install sections point at the
; same CopyFiles section so the file list cannot drift between targets.
;
; usbport.sys gets no service and no registry entry on either target: it loads
; because xhci98.sys imports USBPORT_RegisterUSBPortDriver from it, and the
; loader resolves that at image load. On Win98 NUSB 3.3 has already placed it
; in SYSTEM32\DRIVERS; on Win2000 SP4 it is native. Neither is copied here.
;
; usbd.sys IS copied, and is the one file whose content differs per target.
; usbhub20.sys imports USBD.SYS on both targets, and nothing on an xHCI-only
; machine ever places it: Microsoft's usb.inf copies it only from the USB 1.1
; sections and NUSB does not ship it at all. Each target therefore gets its own
; OS's build, carried on the media under a distinct name (usbd98.sys /
; usbd2k.sys) and selected by the install section that runs, so neither engine
; can reach the other's file. See docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Carrying a
; per-target usbd.sys", and docs\contributing\lessons.md, "usbhub20.sys bugchecks Win2000".
;
; Both halves are transcribed from the shipping reference that installs the
; equivalent EHCI miniport on that target, not written from memory:
;   9x half - NUSB 3.3 USB2.INF, sections [EHCI] / [EHCI.AddReg]
;   NT half - Windows 2000 SP4 I386\USB.IN_, sections [EHCI.NT] /
;             [EHCI.NT.Services] / [EHCI.AddService]
; See docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "The INF must carry both install paths" and
; "Win98 INF-parser traps". scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 enforces the rules
; this file is built to satisfy.

[Version]
Signature="$CHICAGO$"
Class=USB
ClassGUID={36FC9E60-C465-11CF-8056-444553540000}
Provider=%Provider%
; **DriverVer is not cosmetic on Windows 2000**: the setup engine
; ranks candidate drivers by date-then-version, so a package whose version does
; not exceed the installed one is declined as not-better and an upgrade over an
; older build cannot pass. Microsoft's own reference says the same
; thing out loud - SP4's [EHCI.NT] carries "DriverVer=2/15/2003,5.1.2600.1" with
; the comment "Date and Ver should be > any preSP4 QFE".
;
; The scheme:
; **one four-part version per released package, bumped in the last field, with
; the date set to the release date and never moving backwards.** It is a package
; version, not a build counter - the deploy loop overwrites the .sys in place and
; never re-runs the INF, so a per-build number would churn for no observer.
; docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Versioning the driver", has the procedure.
;
; **The major version says whether the package is a final release.** Everything
; before 1.0.0.0 was a 0.x pre-release: cut, published under releases\ and
; installed on real machines exactly like any other, and carrying no claim of
; being finished. What is cut and published is the line below and the newest
; entry in releases\history.md; this comment deliberately names no version,
; because it once named one for two cuts after that stopped being true (pre-cut
; audit, finding C2).
;
; It must equal src\xhci_version.h, which is the one place the version and the
; release date are edited. An INF is a data file setup reads and cannot include
; a header, so this literal is checked against that one instead:
; scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 reads the header as the authority and refuses
; both halves disagreeing. That gate reads *sources*, so it cannot see the one
; copy an installed machine actually reads - the version compiled into the .sys.
; That is scripts\package\make-package.ps1's check, against the binary it
; stages.
DriverVer=08/30/2026,1.0.0.0

; **`Provider` and the manufacturer are two different fields and this INF used
; to fill both from one string.** They mean different things: `[Version]`'s
; Provider is who supplied the *driver* (Driver tab, "Driver Provider"), and the
; key below is the manufacturer of the *device* as this driver reports it
; (General tab, "Manufacturer", stored as `Mfg`). Since the identity split the first is
; a person and the second is the project, which is the honest split - this
; driver binds by class code, so the silicon is Intel's or AMD's or Renesas's
; and naming anyone here as its maker would be false whatever string is chosen.
;
; It also un-confounds a measurement: `docs/contributing/build-and-test.md`
; recorded `Provider=` as feeding *both* fields, which could not be told apart
; while one string filled both slots. A fresh install now separates them.
[Manufacturer]
%Mfg%=XhciModels

; PCI\CC_0C0330 is the xHCI class-code compatible ID - the exact analog of the
; PCI\CC_0C0320 catch-all both reference INFs use for EHCI. Nothing else on
; either target claims it - measured, the device sits unclaimed at Code 28 on
; Windows 98 and Code 1 on Windows 2000 - so a compatible-ID match needs no
; vendor-specific entry to outrank.
[XhciModels]
%XhciDesc%=Xhci.Dev,PCI\CC_0C0330

[SourceDisksNames]
1=%DiskName%,xhci98.sys,,

; usbd98.sys and usbd2k.sys are the two OS builds of the same usbd.sys, kept
; apart on the media by name. The names are the media's, not the target's: both
; are copied to SYSTEM32\DRIVERS as usbd.sys.
[SourceDisksFiles]
xhci98.sys=1
xhci98.inf=1
usbd98.sys=1
usbd2k.sys=1
usbhub98.sys=1

; Dirid 10 + System32\Drivers, never dirid 12: 12 resolves to
; \Windows\System\Iosubsys on Win98 and would install the driver to the wrong
; directory with no error. %12% appears once below, in ServiceBinary, inside a
; section Win98 never reads.
[DestinationDirs]
DefaultDestDir=10
Xhci.CopyFiles=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyW98=10,System32\Drivers
Xhci.CopyW2K=10,System32\Drivers
Inf.CopyFiles=17

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Windows 98 SE (undecorated - Win98 ignores decorated section names)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[Xhci.Dev]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg,Xhci.AddReg.Global
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW98

; The device's whole 9x registry footprint. NUSB's EHCI install adds a third,
; cosmetic EnumPropPages value; this file deliberately omits it, and its
; Win2000 EnumPropPages32/Controller counterparts, because the property-page
; DLLs (sysclass.dll, usbui.dll) are placed by USB installs that never ran on
; an xHCI-only machine - the same absent-dependency shape as the missing
; usbd.sys. They affect no binding and no load.
[Xhci.AddReg]
HKR,,DevLoader,,*NTKERN
HKR,,NTMPDriver,,xhci98.sys
HKR,,XhciLogVerbosity,0x00010001,0
HKR,,XhciLogDebugView,0x00010001,0

; This value lives in its own section because TWO install paths must deliver
; it - see the block below for why one is not enough.
[Xhci.AddReg.Global]
HKLM,System\CurrentControlSet\Services\USB,DisableSelectiveSuspend,0x00010001,1

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; DisableSelectiveSuspend - the only value here that is NOT ours
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; **It is written from two sections on purpose, and that is a measurement
; rather than belt-and-braces.** [Xhci.Dev] delivers it on a device install;
; [DefaultInstall] delivers it on a right-click Install, which touches no
; device. The second route exists because on Windows 98 **every**
; update-over-an-existing-install bugchecks the machine at 0028:C00312EE -
; stopping the running driver IS that fault - so an upgrade can abort before its
; registry phase, and a value carried only by the device install would then
; never arrive on any machine that already had this driver. Measured:
; after an upgrade that crashed, the new binary was
; running and the new INF was cached, and Services\USB did not exist.
;
; **Windows 98 only, and deliberately not on the NT path.** Without this, a
; device attached after the bus goes quiet is detected by nothing until the user
; presses Refresh in Device Manager. NUSB's
; usbport.sys idle-suspends the controller within about half a second of the
; last transfer, and a halted xHC cannot report a port change: the spec gates
; Port Status Change Event generation on HCHalted = '0' (p.322) and says EINT
; and PCD generate no interrupt (p.399), so unlike EHCI - which re-arms
; USBINTR.PCD across its halt - there is no interrupt this driver could leave
; enabled. The fix is therefore to stop the suspend happening, not to wake from
; it.
;
; usbport reads this itself, with RtlQueryRegistryValues(RelativeTo = Services,
; L"usb", ...) alongside UsbBIOSx and DisableCcDetect - read out of NUSB's
; USBPORT.SYS at VA 0x11DBE, the table entry naming L"DisableSelectiveSuspend"
; at 0x11E15. **Measured on the 2a VM**: without it, one
; SuspendController inside seconds and a hot-plug is invisible; with it,
; SuspendController never fires at all, USBCMD reads 0x00000005 (R/S set) and a
; hot-plugged keyboard enumerates on its own - SlotsEnabled 1, DevicesAddressed
; 1, wizard raised with no Refresh.
;
; **Three honest consequences, none of them hidden**:
;
;  1. It is a **global** value under Services\USB, not a per-device one, so it
;     changes behaviour for every controller that usbport drives on the machine,
;     Microsoft's EHCI included. On the deployment population - xHCI-only
;     machines where this driver is the entire USB stack - that is precisely the
;     intent. It is stated in docs\using\release-notes.md rather than left for a user
;     to discover.
;  2. The controller therefore **never idles**, which costs a little power. That
;     is the trade this target wants, and it is the same trade Microsoft's own
;     HcDisableSelectiveSuspend exists to let an administrator make.
;  3. **It outlives the devnode**, because it is outside the devnode's key - so
;     removing the device does not take it with it, and this package cannot know
;     whether something else wanted it. The derived footprint
;     (scripts\inf-gate\expected-footprint.txt) reads `remove` for this row and
;     that is correct: that column answers "what did this install write", which
;     is unconditional here. **What uninstall actually leaves behind is a
;     measurement, not a derivation** - docs\contributing\runs\run-11v.md stage B4 takes it, and
;     docs\using\release-notes.md states the answer.
;
; **The NT path deliberately omits it**: Windows 2000's native usbport never
; idle-suspends this controller - measured on that target - so writing it there
; would change behaviour for no benefit. A value that differs between the two
; paths is normally the failure shape the INF gate exists to catch, which is why
; this one carries its reason here and a gate rule of its own.

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The two log values, on both install paths
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; **Task 13-L.2 made these DWORDs and retired XhciLogFile**, which was a
; REG_SZ whose path was its own enable. The file sink went with it, and so did
; the driver's three Zw* imports - not because they were dangerous but because
; that sink was never observed to write a byte outside a virtual machine, on
; either target. The log file did not disappear: XHCISNAP writes it from ring 3
; now, and names it from the command line.
;
;   XhciLogVerbosity  the 0-4 ladder, and the WHOLE switch:
;                       0  off outright - the PassThru read channel answers
;                          exactly MP_STATUS_NOT_SUPPORTED and nothing can be
;                          read off the machine.  The default.
;                       1  the channel engaged, counter block only; ring off
;                       2  + the note ring - this is the log channel
;                       3  + the PORTSC table in XHCISNAP's companion text
;                       4  + kernel-address records in the note ring, which
;                          the companion text then prints; the bench level.
;                          The raw dump (.BIN extension, .PSC PORTSC) is
;                          whole at every level from 1 up; only the ring's
;                          content and the companion text are gated.
;                     The channel is present in EVERY build flavour; what the
;                     flavour decides is how much there is to read.
;   XhciLogDebugView  an EMISSION switch and nothing else: it hands the ring to
;                     DbgPrint at the PASSIVE flush.
;
; **There were three values until the snapshot-value merge**, the third being
; XhciLogSnapshot, which consented to the read channel. It was a pure consent
; bit - the channel serves everything or nothing, so it carried no width - and
; consent nests inside depth, so it became rung 0 of the ladder above. A machine
; installed from an older INF still carries the value; the driver no longer
; reads it, which costs a start nothing.
;
; **Both default to 0**, and that is a polarity decision rather than
; caution: the safe configuration is the default and the convenience is the
; thing asked for by name. An absent value is 0 too, and is not an error -
; a machine whose INF never ran and a machine whose owner set nothing behave
; identically, and the driver starts normally on both.
;
; The driver reads both through usbport's own
; UsbPortGetMiniportRegistryKeyValue service with BOOL = TRUE, which is
; IoOpenDeviceRegistryKey's PLUGPLAY_REGKEY_DRIVER - the **software (driver)
; key**, which is what a plain AddReg under an install section writes and which
; is where the shipping usbehci.sys reads its own parameter from. Derived from
; both shipping usbport builds and both shipping usbehci builds; see
; docs\usb-xhci-info\usbport-miniport-abi.md section 6. There is no Set
; counterpart at any offset in any build, so nothing here is ever written back
; by the driver - XHCISNAP sets these from user mode.
;
; Both are written on BOTH paths, and that is the point rather than
; symmetry: a value present on only one install path is exactly the failure
; shape the INF gate exists to catch, and scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 checks
; the 9x and the NT section separately for each of them.
;
; 0x00010001 is FLG_ADDREG_TYPE_DWORD. It is not an NT-only spelling - NUSB's
; own USBSTOR.INF writes "HKR,,DriverFlags,0x00010001,0x00000002" from an
; undecorated section, i.e. through Win98's 16-bit engine. **Every one of these
; is now a DWORD**, which retires the one line that was FLG_ADDREG_TYPE_SZ and
; with it an unmeasured question: how Win98's 16-bit engine writes an empty
; REG_SZ was never established, and the driver was written so that it did not
; have to be. It no longer arises.
;
; **An out-of-range XhciLogVerbosity is refused, not clamped**: the driver falls
; back to 0 and records that it did, so a dump never reports a tier nobody asked
; for. A 7 typed here does not become level 4 - and since 0 is now off outright,
; a refusal SHUTS the channel rather than opening it at some other rung, which
; is the right direction for a refusal to fail in.
;
; **Turning it on is not a registry edit any more**, which is the whole point
; of the change: XHCISNAP sets it from a DOS box, finds the per-machine
; software key itself, and the published sequence is enable, restart, reproduce,
; XHCISNAP -o C:\NAME. A ready-made .reg cannot ship because that key is
; per machine, and a Device Manager property page is not an option either -
; its 9x half would have to be a 16-bit DLL with no programmatic install path
; (docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "No programmatic install path on Win98").
;
; **The enable step is also the access control, and that is stated rather than
; hoped.** This driver cannot tighten the door it answers through: usbport owns
; the device object, hardcodes \DosDevices\HCD<n>, completes IRP_MJ_CREATE with
; no work, and the IOCTL is FILE_ANY_ACCESS. So while the channel is enabled,
; any local user who can open that name can read this driver's diagnostic state
; - counters, ring notes, PORTSC, and kernel addresses (the raw window carries
; the whole miniport extension at any level from 1 up; only XHCISNAP's
; plain-text companion withholds them below level 4); no user data and no
; other process's memory. Writing this value needs Administrators on
; Windows 2000 and nothing at all on Windows 98, which has no user boundary.
; Run XHCISNAP -disable after sending a capture.
;
; **The cost of the defaults is real and is paid deliberately.** The ring costs
; 16 KB of the miniport extension whatever this says; what the default saves is
; the append sites' cost at real interrupt rates on Windows 98 metal, which is
; UNMEASURED - and rung 1 is what that argument buys, since it engages the
; channel without turning the append sites on. An end user who has not asked for
; a log has no channel open at all.

[Xhci.AddReg.NT]
HKR,,XhciLogVerbosity,0x00010001,0
HKR,,XhciLogDebugView,0x00010001,0

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Windows 2000 SP4 (.NTx86 - most specific match wins on NT)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[Xhci.Dev.NTx86]
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.NT
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW2K

[Xhci.Dev.NTx86.Services]
AddService=xhci98,0x00000002,Xhci.AddService

[Xhci.AddService]
DisplayName=%XhciSvcDesc%
ServiceType=1                       ; SERVICE_KERNEL_DRIVER
StartType=3                         ; SERVICE_DEMAND_START
ErrorControl=1                      ; SERVICE_ERROR_NORMAL
ServiceBinary=%12%\xhci98.sys
LoadOrderGroup=Base

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Shared file lists
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

; The third field is the temporary name. Reinstalling over a loaded
; xhci98.sys is the normal case in the deploy loop; without it Win98 has no
; way to replace the file and can leave the previous binary in place.
;
; MEASURED (first fresh install on 2a): Win98 does the
; copy-through correctly - the loaded binary really is the new one - but it
; **leaves xhci98.tmp in System32\Drivers afterwards and lists it in the
; device's own Driver File Details**, beside xhci98.sys, NTKERN.vxd and
; USBD.SYS.
;
; NARROWED: those are two independent facts,
; and only the first is about the disk. A fresh install onto a pristine image -
; nothing for the destination to be in use *by* - still **lists** xhci98.tmp in
; Driver File Details while `dir /a ...\xhci98.*` answers `1 file(s)`,
; XHCI98.SYS alone. So the list is derived from this CopyFiles entry's third
; field rather than from the filesystem, and it is evidence of neither presence
; nor absence. The residue itself is an *upgrade* artifact
; (docs\contributing\runs\run-11v.md). That is cosmetic and a small disk leak
; rather than a load problem, and it is NOT a reason to drop the third field:
; without it the replace fails outright, which is the worse defect.
; Do not read the .tmp in that list as the driver having loaded
; the wrong file - confirm which binary is live from the trace's
; `DriverEntry (built ...)` line, as always.
[Xhci.CopyFiles]
xhci98.sys,,xhci98.tmp

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The one file that differs per target: usbd.sys
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Field order is Destination,Source,Temporary,Flags (Oney p.386): the second
; field is "the name of the file as it exists on the distribution media if
; that name is different from the Destination name", which is what keeps the
; two builds apart. The media carries usbd98.sys and usbd2k.sys; each section
; names exactly one of them, and each section is reachable from exactly one
; install path - so there is no ordering, version comparison, or engine
; behaviour that could let Win98's build land on Win2000 or the reverse. Only
; the section that runs can name a source file at all.
;
; Flag 16 = COPYFLG_NO_OVERWRITE, "do not copy if file exists on target"
; (C:\NTDDK\inc\SETUPAPI.H). Presence is the entire requirement - usbhub20.sys
; needs the import to resolve - so an existing usbd.sys is left strictly alone
; whatever its version. That is deliberately stronger than the version-based
; flags: 4 (COPYFLG_NOVERSIONCHECK) would overwrite a newer post-SP4 or
; hotfix build, and 32 (COPYFLG_NO_VERSION_DIALOG) still replaces an older
; one for no benefit. The reference INFs use decimal in this field
; (NUSB _NUSB.INF: "usbstor.sys,,,4"), so this does too.
;
; If a future engine were found to ignore the flag, the Win98 path would still
; be safe by construction: usbd98.sys is bit-for-bit the file Win98 SE's own
; media ships, so an unwanted overwrite there rewrites the file with itself.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; usbhub.sys - WINDOWS 98 ONLY, and the asymmetry is the point
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Added by batch 13-E, which found it missing on real hardware.
; usbhub.sys is Windows 98's COMPOSITE PARENT driver: USB.INF binds a
; multi-interface device to it, and without it every composite device on the
; machine stops at `USB Composite Device` with **Code 2** - "the NTKERN.VXD
; device loader(s) for this device could not load the device driver", which is
; matched-but-unloadable rather than Code 1's unmatched.
;
; It is the same absent-dependency shape as usbd.sys and it has the same cause.
; Windows 98 ships USB.INF on every install but copies the USB driver *files*
; only when setup detects a USB controller, and an xHCI-only machine looks
; empty to Win98 setup. So the INF is present, the devnode gets named, and the
; file is not there. Measured on the ThinkPad E460 by batch 13-E: absent, every
; composite device dead including a plain two-interface HID keyboard that would
; not type; the file copied in by hand, and the same machine drives them all.
; The control was a ThinkPad X61 - same Windows 98 SE, same NUSB 3.3, no xHCI -
; where the file is present because setup found its UHCI controller.
;
; **This one is NOT delivered to Windows 2000, and that is deliberate rather
; than an omission.** On Win2000 `usbhub.sys` is the name of the OS's own USB
; 1.1 hub driver, and its composite parent is a different file entirely
; (usbccgp.sys). Delivering Win98 SE's 4.10.2222 build toward that target would
; be wrong even with flag 16 protecting it. Win2000 also does not need the
; help: setup copies %SystemRoot%\Driver Cache\i386\driver.cab
; unconditionally, whatever hardware it found, and PnP pulls in-box drivers
; from it with no media prompt - a mechanism Windows 98 has no guaranteed
; equivalent of. That reasoning is **structural and unverified**: every
; Windows 2000 vehicle in this project has an EHCI, so the xHCI-only case
; cannot be observed on that target - and no bare-metal Windows 2000 vehicle
; exists in this project either, so it is published as a limitation. See
; docs/contributing/roadmap.md, Phase 13's checkpoint.
;
; scripts\inf-gate\check-inf.ps1 enforces the asymmetry in BOTH directions -
; the Win98 path must deliver it and the Win2000 path must not - so this cannot
; be "fixed" later by someone making the two paths look symmetrical.

[Xhci.CopyW98]
usbd.sys,usbd98.sys,,16
usbhub.sys,usbhub98.sys,,16

[Xhci.CopyW2K]
usbd.sys,usbd2k.sys,,16

[Inf.CopyFiles]
xhci98.inf

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Right-click Install - pre-stages the files with no device present
; ------------------------------------------------------------------

[DefaultInstall]
CopyFiles=Inf.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW98
AddReg=Xhci.AddReg.Global

; Win2000's setup engine keeps its own copy of an OEM INF as oemN.inf; copying
; this one into %17% as well would leave two INFs claiming PCI\CC_0C0330.
[DefaultInstall.NTx86]
CopyFiles=Xhci.CopyFiles,Xhci.CopyW2K

; ASCII only, and that is a parser requirement rather than a style
; note: [Strings] is read by **both** engines and this file is ANSI/CRLF for
; Win98's 16-bit one, so a curly quote or an en dash here is the class of change
; that makes that engine report nothing at all
; (docs\contributing\build-and-test.md, "Win98 INF-parser traps").
;
; %XhciDesc% is what Device Manager shows, what the "Have Disk" list shows during
; the documented install, and what a user reads back in a bug report - on the
; a machine that also has EHCI it appears beside Microsoft's own entries. That
; marker is
; therefore load-bearing and is not to be dropped for tidiness; the rest is the
; eXtensible-host-controller form the hardware actually has a name for.
;
; "USB 2.0" leads the string deliberately, and is the second load-bearing part.
; An xHCI controller is what a user associates with USB 3.0, and this driver
; drives one - but it manages only the USB 2.0 protocol ports and leaves the
; SuperSpeed ones unpowered by design (AGENTS.md, "Port Strategy"). A name that
; says only "eXtensible Host Controller" invites the reading that SuperSpeed
; devices will run at SuperSpeed, which is the one expectation this project
; cannot meet and has no way to explain in a Device Manager row. Keep it first,
; where it survives a truncated column.
;
; **Changing these strings is not observable on an existing devnode.** Both
; engines cache DriverDesc in the device's software key at install time, so an
; already-installed machine keeps the old string across a reboot, and the deploy
; loop's in-place .sys overwrite does not exercise this file at all. The
; observation is a fresh install, or an uninstall-and-reinstall, on each target.
[Strings]
Provider="Yeo Kheng Meng"
Mfg="xHCI98 Project"
DiskName="xhci98 xHCI Miniport Disk"
XhciDesc="USB 2.0 eXtensible Host Controller (xhci98)"
XhciSvcDesc="xHCI USB 2.0 Host Controller Miniport (xhci98)"
